.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt DTFL_STREAM_STATE_MACHINE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_stream_state_machine
.Nd thread per-key state across records
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/timely_ops.h
.Ft dtfl_stream *
.Fo dtfl_stream_state_machine
.Fa "dtfl_stream *st"
.Fa "size_t state_size"
.Fa "dtfl_state_fn step"
.Fa "const dtfl_type *out_type"
.Fa "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dtfl_stream_state_machine
keeps a state of
.Fa state_size
bytes for each key and applies
.Fn step
to each
.Pq key , value
record in time order.
The records of
.Fa st
are pairs whose first field is the key; the input is exchanged by the
key's hash, so all records with the same key reach the same worker.
.Pp
.Fn step
receives the key and value bytes, the key's state, and a
.Vt dtfl_emit
handle through which it writes output records of
.Fa out_type .
It returns
.Dv true
to deregister the key's state once it is no longer helpful.
A record whose time the input frontier has not yet reached is stashed
and applied once the frontier passes it; a record at an already
complete time is applied immediately.
Within one timestamp the system may reorder records, so
.Fn step
must not depend on intra-timestamp order.
The context
.Fa ctx
is released by its destructor when the operator is dropped (PLAN D5).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_stream_state_machine
returns the stream of emitted records, carrying
.Fa out_type .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* Running sum per key, emitting (key, sum) on each record. */
static bool run(void *c, const void *k, size_t kl,
    const void *v, size_t vl, void *st, dtfl_emit *o) {
	*(uint64_t *)st += dtfl_get_u64(v);
	/* emit (k, *st) */ return false;
}
dtfl_stream *out = dtfl_stream_state_machine(s, sizeof(uint64_t),
    run, pair_type, DTFL_NOCTX);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_stream_aggregate 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7
.Sh HISTORY
This is the C form of timely's
.Vt StateMachine
trait.
It is public at the pinned release
.Pq Li timely-v0.31.0
and was removed upstream afterward (#818); the port keeps the pinned
behavior.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
The streams and the operator belong to the thread that runs the worker
and must not be used from another thread (PLAN section 5).
